module

public import Mathlib.Algebra.Category.ModuleCat.Sheaf.PullbackFree
public import StacksFrontier.CategoryTheory.Sites.Cohomology.HigherDirectImage.ExtAlongAnExactFunctor
public import StacksFrontier.CategoryTheory.Sites.Cohomology.LowerShriek.DerivedAdjunction
public import StacksFrontier.CategoryTheory.Sites.Cohomology.Modules.CohomologyOfModules
public import StacksCore.Tactic.StacksAttribute

/-!
# Cohomology of sheaves of modules does not change on a bigger site

Let `f : C ⥤ D` be a continuous and cocontinuous functor of sites, let `O` be a sheaf of rings on
`(D, K)` and let `g^{-1}O` be its restriction along `f`. Restriction of sheaves of modules
`g^* : Mod(O) ⥤ Mod(g^{-1}O)` is then exact and has the lower-shriek functor `g_!` as a left
adjoint. If moreover `C` has a terminal object whose image is terminal in `D`, then restriction
does not change the cohomology of sheaves of modules:

`H^p(C, g^*F) ≅ H^p(D, F)`,

naturally in the sheaf of `O`-modules `F`, where `H^p(-, -)` is `Ext^p` out of the structure
sheaf viewed as a module over itself.

This is the module half of **Stacks 03YU** — the sentence "the same holds for sheaves of modules"
which that lemma and its consumers attach to the abelian statement — at *independent universes*:
the two sites have objects and morphisms in arbitrary universes, with the sheaf of rings and the
module sections in the universe generated by them. Geometric sites are of that shape: their
objects live one universe above their morphisms, and they are not small.

## Main definitions

* `Stacks.Cohomology.BiggerSiteModules.moduleFunctorH`: the cohomology of sheaves of modules on a
  site, as `Ext^p` out of the structure sheaf. `extZeroEquivSections` identifies it in degree zero
  with the compatible global sections, which is what makes it the cohomology of the source.

## Main results

* `Stacks.Cohomology.BiggerSiteModules.moduleCohomologyIsoOfAcyclic`: the comparison of module
  cohomology along the functor of sites, from acyclicity of the restriction of an injective sheaf
  of modules.
* `Stacks.Cohomology.BiggerSiteModules.moduleCohomologyIsoOfUnitIsoOfAcyclic`: the same
  comparison with the two terminal objects weakened to invertibility of the comparison
  `g_! g^{-1}O ⟶ O` of unit objects, which is all they are a route to.
* `CategoryTheory.Functor.final_of_isTerminal_obj`: a functor carrying a terminal object to a
  terminal object is final.
* `CategoryTheory.essentiallySmall_of_le`: essential smallness in one universe gives essential
  smallness in any larger one. Both are general statements about categories, recorded here
  because they are the two inputs the comparison needs and Mathlib has neither: the first
  identifies `g_! O_C` with `O_D`, the second moves the essential smallness of a geometric site
  from the universe of its sections up to the universe the module cohomology lives in.

## The acyclicity hypothesis, and why it is a hypothesis

The comparison is proved from the universality of `Ext` as a `δ`-functor (Stacks 010T and 010U),
in the form `Stacks.Cohomology.ExtComparison.extFunctorObjIsoCompOfIsoOfAcyclic`: the two
`δ`-functors `Ext^p_{Mod(O)}(g_! O_C, -)` and `g^* ⋙ Ext^p_{Mod(g^{-1}O)}(O_C, -)` on `Mod(O)`
agree in degree zero by the adjunction `g_! ⊣ g^*`, and the extension to every degree needs the
second to be universal, that is, to vanish in positive degrees on the restriction of an injective
sheaf of modules.

That vanishing is carried below as the explicit hypothesis `hacyclic`. It is the module form of
**Stacks 0D6X** and it is *not* available as preservation of injective objects by `g^*`: that
would require the left adjoint `g_!` to be exact, which fails for the inclusion of a subsite
with no fibre products. The whole point of taking the acyclicity route rather than
`Stacks.Cohomology.ExtComparison.extFunctorObjIsoCompOfAdjunction` is that acyclicity is the only
hypothesis available there.

## Implementation notes

The identification `g_! O_C ≅ O_D` of the unit objects is Mathlib's
`SheafOfModules.pullbackObjUnitToUnit`, which is an isomorphism as soon as the functor of sites is
*final*. Finality is where the terminal objects enter, through
`CategoryTheory.Functor.final_of_isTerminal_obj`: the comma category of morphisms `d ⟶ f(c)` has a
terminal object, namely the unique morphism to `f` of the terminal object of `C`, and a category
with a terminal object is connected. This replaces the abelian file's
`Stacks.Cohomology.BiggerSiteGlobal.lowerShriekConstantSheafIso`, which identifies `g_! ℤ_C` with
`ℤ_D` by uniqueness of left adjoints; the module unit is the structure sheaf rather than a
constant sheaf, so it is not the image of a left adjoint and that argument is unavailable.

The sheaf of rings and the module sections live in the universe `max u v w` generated by the two
sites and a free universe `w`. This is forced, not a convenience: the unit object of
`Mod(O)` is `O` itself, so the module universe cannot be smaller than the ring universe, while
the Grothendieck abelian structure on `Mod(O)` — hence enough injectives and the existence of
`Ext` — needs the module universe to contain the site. At that universe every statement used here
is available for an arbitrary site, by
`StacksFrontier/CategoryTheory/Sites/Cohomology/Modules/CohomologyOfModules.lean`.

Exactness of restriction and the adjunction `g_! ⊣ g^*` are taken from
`StacksFrontier/CategoryTheory/Sites/Cohomology/LowerShriek/DerivedAdjunction.lean`, whose
`Stacks.Cohomology.DerivedLowerShriek.restrictionModules` is the same functor as Mathlib's
`SheafOfModules.pushforward` at the identity structure map. Existence of `g_!` on sheaves of
modules is the first sentence of **Stacks 0789** and is carried, as it is there, as the instance
hypothesis `[(SheafOfModules.pushforward (idRingMap J K f O)).IsRightAdjoint]`; it is discharged
by search on a small site and, for the subsite of a stack, by
`Stacks.Sheaves.ToposPullback.isRightAdjoint_restrictionModules`.

## References

* [Stacks Project, Tag 010T](https://stacks.math.columbia.edu/tag/010T)
* [Stacks Project, Tag 010U](https://stacks.math.columbia.edu/tag/010U)
* [Stacks Project, Tag 03YU](https://stacks.math.columbia.edu/tag/03YU)
* [Stacks Project, Tag 0789](https://stacks.math.columbia.edu/tag/0789)
* [Stacks Project, Tag 0D6X](https://stacks.math.columbia.edu/tag/0D6X)
-/

@[expose] public noncomputable section

open CategoryTheory CategoryTheory.Limits Opposite CategoryTheory.Abelian
open Stacks.Cohomology.DerivedLowerShriek

namespace CategoryTheory

universe t t' v₁ v₂ u₁ u₂

/-- **Essential smallness passes to a larger universe.** A category equivalent to a small
category in `Type t` is equivalent to a small category in `Type (max t t')`.

Both halves of `CategoryTheory.essentiallySmall_iff` — smallness of the skeleton and local
smallness — are smallness of a type, which `small_lift` moves up. -/
theorem essentiallySmall_of_le {C : Type u₁} [Category.{v₁} C] [EssentiallySmall.{t} C] :
    EssentiallySmall.{max t t'} C := by
  rw [essentiallySmall_iff] at *
  obtain ⟨hskel, hloc⟩ := ‹Small.{t} (Skeleton C) ∧ LocallySmall.{t} C›
  exact ⟨small_lift.{u₁, t', t} _, ⟨fun _ _ ↦ small_lift.{v₁, t', t} _⟩⟩

namespace Functor

/-- **A functor carrying a terminal object to a terminal object is final.** If `e` is terminal in
`C` and `f e` is terminal in `D`, then for every `d : D` the comma category of morphisms
`d ⟶ f(c)` is connected, because the unique morphism `d ⟶ f(e)` is a terminal object of it: a
morphism to it is a morphism to `e` in `C`, of which there is exactly one, and the compatibility
square commutes automatically because `f e` is terminal.

This is the general statement behind the identification of `g_! O_C` with `O_D` in
`Stacks.Cohomology.BiggerSiteModules.moduleCohomologyIsoOfAcyclic`; Mathlib has finality from an
adjunction (`CategoryTheory.Functor.final_of_adjunction`) but not from terminal objects. -/
theorem final_of_isTerminal_obj {C : Type u₁} [Category.{v₁} C] {D : Type u₂} [Category.{v₂} D]
    (f : C ⥤ D) {e : C} (he : IsTerminal e) (hfe : IsTerminal (f.obj e)) : f.Final where
  out d :=
    let t : StructuredArrow d f := StructuredArrow.mk (hfe.from d)
    have hto : ∀ g : StructuredArrow d f, g ⟶ t := fun g =>
      StructuredArrow.homMk (he.from g.right) (hfe.hom_ext _ _)
    @zigzag_isConnected _ _ ⟨t⟩ fun g g' =>
      Zigzag.trans (Zigzag.of_hom (hto g)) (Zigzag.of_inv (hto g'))

end Functor

end CategoryTheory

namespace Stacks.Cohomology.BiggerSiteModules

universe w v u

section Cohomology

variable {C : Type u} [Category.{v} C] (J : GrothendieckTopology C)
  (O : Sheaf J RingCat.{max u v w})

/-- **The cohomology of sheaves of modules on a site**: `H^p(C, F) = Ext^p(O, F)`, the `Ext`-groups
out of the structure sheaf viewed as a module over itself.

This is the module counterpart of Mathlib's `CategoryTheory.Sheaf.functorH`, which is `Ext^p` out
of the constant sheaf on `ℤ` in the category of abelian sheaves. In degree zero it is the
compatible global sections, by `extZeroEquivSections`. -/
@[sga "4 V 2.1.2"]
abbrev moduleFunctorH (p : ℕ) :
    SheafOfModules.{max u v w} O ⥤ AddCommGrpCat.{max u v w} :=
  extFunctorObj (SheafOfModules.unit O) p

/-- **Module cohomology in degree zero is the compatible global sections.** `Ext^0(O, F)` is
`Hom(O, F)`, which is the module of global sections of `F`.

This is what identifies `moduleFunctorH` as the cohomology of the source: it is a `δ`-functor
whose degree-zero part is global sections, hence the right derived functor of global sections. -/
def extZeroEquivSections (F : SheafOfModules.{max u v w} O) :
    Ext (SheafOfModules.unit O) F 0 ≃ F.sections :=
  Ext.addEquiv₀.toEquiv.trans (SheafOfModules.unitHomEquiv F)

end Cohomology

section Comparison

variable {C : Type u} [Category.{v} C] {D : Type u} [Category.{v} D]
  (J : GrothendieckTopology C) (K : GrothendieckTopology D) (f : C ⥤ D)
  [f.IsContinuous J K] [f.IsCocontinuous J K] (O : Sheaf K RingCat.{max u v w})
  [(SheafOfModules.pushforward.{max u v w} (F := f) (idRingMap J K f O)).IsRightAdjoint]

/-! The two sites are arbitrary, so sheafification of abelian presheaves with sections in the
module universe is not available by search; it holds for every site at that universe, by
`Stacks.Cohomology.SiteModules.site_hasSheafify_addCommGrp`, and is installed here with the
universe pinned because left free it is a metavariable the elaborator cannot solve. These are the
inputs through which exactness of restriction of modules is obtained. -/

/-- Sheafification of abelian presheaves on the source site `(C, J)`. -/
local instance hasSheafify_source : HasSheafify J AddCommGrpCat.{max u v w} :=
  Stacks.Cohomology.SiteModules.site_hasSheafify_addCommGrp.{u, v, w} (J := J)

/-- On the source site `(C, J)`, the morphisms of abelian presheaves inverted by sheafification
are the locally bijective ones. -/
local instance wEqualsLocallyBijective_source :
    J.WEqualsLocallyBijective AddCommGrpCat.{max u v w} :=
  Stacks.Cohomology.SiteModules.site_wEqualsLocallyBijective_addCommGrp.{u, v, w} (J := J)

/-- Sheafification of abelian presheaves on the target site `(D, K)`. -/
local instance hasSheafify_target : HasSheafify K AddCommGrpCat.{max u v w} :=
  Stacks.Cohomology.SiteModules.site_hasSheafify_addCommGrp.{u, v, w} (J := K)

/-- On the target site `(D, K)`, the morphisms of abelian presheaves inverted by sheafification
are the locally bijective ones. -/
local instance wEqualsLocallyBijective_target :
    K.WEqualsLocallyBijective AddCommGrpCat.{max u v w} :=
  Stacks.Cohomology.SiteModules.site_wEqualsLocallyBijective_addCommGrp.{u, v, w} (J := K)

/-- **Stacks 03YU for sheaves of modules, from the comparison of unit objects and acyclicity.**
Let `f : C ⥤ D` be a continuous and cocontinuous functor of sites and `O` a sheaf of rings on
`(D, K)`. Suppose that `g_!` carries the structure sheaf `g^{-1}O` of the source to the structure
sheaf `O` of the target — that is, that Mathlib's comparison map
`SheafOfModules.pullbackObjUnitToUnit` is invertible — and that the restriction along `f` of an
injective sheaf of `O`-modules has vanishing higher `Ext` out of the structure sheaf. Then for
every degree `p`

`H^p(C, g^*F) ≅ H^p(D, F)`

naturally in the sheaf of `O`-modules `F`.

This is `moduleCohomologyIsoOfAcyclic` with the two terminal objects replaced by the one
isomorphism they are a route to. In that statement terminality enters only through
`CategoryTheory.Functor.final_of_isTerminal_obj`, which makes `f` final, which makes
`pullbackObjUnitToUnit` invertible; and a terminal object is unavailable for a full subsite of
the site of an algebraic stack with a nontrivial automorphism, by
`CategoryTheory.Functor.IsFiberedInGroupoids.not_isTerminal_of_faithful_of_ne_id`, while the
comparison of unit objects — the degree-zero case of the statement — can still hold. -/
def moduleCohomologyIsoOfUnitIsoOfAcyclic
    (hunit : IsIso (SheafOfModules.pullbackObjUnitToUnit.{_, _, _, _, max u v w}
      (idRingMap J K f O)))
    (hacyclic : ∀ I : SheafOfModules.{max u v w} O, Injective I → ∀ (n : ℕ)
      (a : Ext (SheafOfModules.unit (inverseImageRing J K f O))
        ((restrictionModules J K f O).obj I) (n + 1)), a = 0)
    (p : ℕ) :
    restrictionModules J K f O ⋙ moduleFunctorH J (inverseImageRing J K f O) p ≅
      moduleFunctorH K O p :=
  haveI := hunit
  (Stacks.Cohomology.ExtComparison.extFunctorObjIsoCompOfIsoOfAcyclic
    (adjunctionModules J K f O)
    (asIso (SheafOfModules.pullbackObjUnitToUnit.{_, _, _, _, max u v w} (idRingMap J K f O)))
    (fun I _ n a ↦ hacyclic I inferInstance n a) p).symm

/-- **Stacks 03YU for sheaves of modules, from acyclicity.** Let `f : C ⥤ D` be a continuous and
cocontinuous functor of sites and `O` a sheaf of rings on `(D, K)`. Suppose `C` has a terminal
object `e` whose image `f e` is terminal in `D`, and suppose that the restriction along `f` of an
injective sheaf of `O`-modules has vanishing higher `Ext` out of the structure sheaf. Then for
every degree `p`

`H^p(C, g^*F) ≅ H^p(D, F)`

naturally in the sheaf of `O`-modules `F`, where `g^*` is restriction of modules along `f` and
`H^p` is `Ext^p` out of the structure sheaf.

The acyclicity hypothesis `hacyclic` is the module form of Stacks 0D6X; see the module
docstring for why it cannot be replaced by preservation of injective objects. The terminal
objects are used only to make `f` final, hence to invert the comparison of unit objects which
`moduleCohomologyIsoOfUnitIsoOfAcyclic` takes as a hypothesis; the term below is that statement's
term with the instance supplied by finality rather than a call to it, because the call forces the
elaborator to unify the two `Ext` universe instantiations and that unification exceeds the
heartbeat budget. -/
def moduleCohomologyIsoOfAcyclic {e : C} (he : IsTerminal e) (hfe : IsTerminal (f.obj e))
    (hacyclic : ∀ I : SheafOfModules.{max u v w} O, Injective I → ∀ (n : ℕ)
      (a : Ext (SheafOfModules.unit (inverseImageRing J K f O))
        ((restrictionModules J K f O).obj I) (n + 1)), a = 0)
    (p : ℕ) :
    restrictionModules J K f O ⋙ moduleFunctorH J (inverseImageRing J K f O) p ≅
      moduleFunctorH K O p :=
  haveI := Functor.final_of_isTerminal_obj f he hfe
  (Stacks.Cohomology.ExtComparison.extFunctorObjIsoCompOfIsoOfAcyclic
    (adjunctionModules J K f O)
    (asIso (SheafOfModules.pullbackObjUnitToUnit.{_, _, _, _, max u v w} (idRingMap J K f O)))
    (fun I _ n a ↦ hacyclic I inferInstance n a) p).symm

end Comparison

end Stacks.Cohomology.BiggerSiteModules

end
